#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9fddf3-6192-507c-a4f4-9269eccdfbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − e^{5})/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1f71da-93f1-53d1-b906-fe647e3f6415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5 − e^{2})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269368} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6dd21d-f760-5403-8093-6e3178d84733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − e^{2})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269369} {:answer/id #uuid "519beaf5-4aef-5c30-ab11-0969e82ead52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} {:answer/id #uuid "deebc8a2-6408-5ed5-aba3-f8a9c5ab1baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5 − e^{5})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519beaf5-4aef-5c30-ab11-0969e82ead52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5270813-7ff6-5a96-8bce-f40597f0f4b6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b12403ef-850e-548f-8484-3ee9e4f60e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-100639" :question/problem "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}(8x)=10.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(8x) & =10 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(8x)} & ={e}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(8x)}=8x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & ={e}^{10} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a28d932-f402-5174-bde9-7f2ed99e09e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269372} {:answer/id #uuid "42c1f69a-6cc4-5bdf-9b61-88089ce4830a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269373} {:answer/id #uuid "e96961f4-f219-5ccf-a566-d6871a76b458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269374} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8dfedf-24d8-570f-89f8-ec5d7ff9c911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269375} {:answer/id #uuid "6a98a64b-4cb6-57c2-a4dd-c6a3c293b945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c1f69a-6cc4-5bdf-9b61-88089ce4830a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269373} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafd877a-9c4f-56b0-af83-4c4d218df360" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274149}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2471a67-c72a-575a-8f10-13bf4266225f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8ba6cd4-1131-522c-8753-6fdbf943b4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{4} − 24)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269377} {:answer/id #uuid "b021478f-7ccf-52db-9d59-f0e93dcd3f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24 − e^{4})/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269378} {:answer/id #uuid "14e318e8-4360-5a5f-91ee-7a41b418ce03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 − e^{4})/(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269379} {:answer/id #uuid "089ff636-8b26-5ef9-b6e5-8e167d7feba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} {:answer/id #uuid "55aec728-0678-5246-8d81-8387f71eabae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{4} − 3)/(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "089ff636-8b26-5ef9-b6e5-8e167d7feba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b7e97a-8b1a-5b32-93f8-9235230da6cd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28439940-5e97-5723-8894-149495e6acc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100724" :question/problem "If $4\\operatorname{ln}x-2=10,$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}x-2 & =10 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}x & =12 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1d3cd0-de12-543e-b63a-a4b64c0d59b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{4} − 24)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269382} {:answer/id #uuid "89ff7114-00f6-55ea-ac78-a5498ab80a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24 − e^{4})/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269383} {:answer/id #uuid "3c89c1d7-cdf2-5724-841c-cb92d35a7616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 − e^{4})/(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269384} {:answer/id #uuid "957b70e5-d57b-539f-91e6-564a7d66690f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{4} − 3)/(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269385} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd5cf58-1af1-5c35-ad4e-2deff3aa57fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fd5cf58-1af1-5c35-ad4e-2deff3aa57fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269386} :answer-field/id #uuid "cffafeb2-f794-59e5-97d6-10c5611be0ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b63ef9f-9d27-55a5-91e1-cd4230085c7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100737" :question/problem "If $11\\operatorname{ln}x-7=15,$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{ln}x-7 & =15 \\\\ 11\\operatorname{ln}x & =22 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x & =2 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x} & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de47d488-5ab0-5164-9190-b4a2606d8d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269387} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f4f8c2-ca96-5aa4-bd60-7956cf72f95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269388} {:answer/id #uuid "95c15df5-e6da-5d92-be19-8466d053f2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269389} {:answer/id #uuid "de82e67e-3117-5f2a-abaf-5b54f61d5e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269390} {:answer/id #uuid "2a65402a-f1b6-537b-bdc7-0078c7ccc236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de47d488-5ab0-5164-9190-b4a2606d8d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269387} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab440b31-1721-50c3-8136-a522d3108266" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "501f700f-e881-502f-a49c-ed376789d61f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100771" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36)={log}_{5}(-16v-28).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & ={log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {v}^{2}+36 & =-16v-28 \\\\ {v}^{2}+16v+64 & =0 \\\\ (v+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}((-8)^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16(-8)-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}(100) & ={log}_{5}(100).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-8$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c943a2e-592b-5f26-ae05-bf5c394e29b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269512} {:answer/id #uuid "55076d03-37b4-5133-8eeb-b36c452b2adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269513} {:answer/id #uuid "7365a605-180c-5bb5-abea-bfe4109cd1d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269514} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d9c0ce-fb61-54eb-9528-b66710176cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269515} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d57fb5-992a-5791-aa4e-1600a216520c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3d57fb5-992a-5791-aa4e-1600a216520c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2b76ae-6c7c-5794-92e5-5a93ac85df73" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ec12c4d-f13a-5615-b8ec-dbfa0807307e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102626" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2-3t}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2-3t}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0c5e990-d8e2-52b7-a00a-a665734185af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269592} {:answer/id #uuid "03490656-6b49-5235-8857-38f9572b036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} {:answer/id #uuid "8443cecd-f0d3-5a5b-8ee1-4123c1337a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269594} {:answer/id #uuid "1d317a91-f6e2-5d97-aaef-9603b88d181a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269595} {:answer/id #uuid "25d67988-1452-5e88-81c9-55463df11832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03490656-6b49-5235-8857-38f9572b036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} :answer-field/id #uuid "185ee15c-5edc-561d-bddd-29feb2e3017a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274193}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "313d80f2-5348-5223-83dd-06c7adf91fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-106719" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}(4x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81d5fa54-0b80-52c1-97d7-6f8fb231f692" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365d86be-fb79-5829-aff2-4776e26b3d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269717} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc78a7a-6c20-51b4-9f85-2f88864026da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}((t+1){e}^{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269718} {:answer/id #uuid "56dcff50-d2e0-5bf5-ab61-92ce6a653f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2((t+1){e}^{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269719} {:answer/id #uuid "73b82d14-bb25-5d9f-a688-c6b51f2e909d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3((t+1){e}^{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269720} {:answer/id #uuid "35564664-b25d-5bef-8901-cdee85405259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-((t+1){e}^{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "365d86be-fb79-5829-aff2-4776e26b3d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269717} :answer-field/id #uuid "972d6d91-fa7a-5e6c-a4ef-7aac2d51c74f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cf72795-fd62-5248-9d55-fccd7b6e1ff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-108995" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=t{e}^{t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebaccbeb-3737-590c-8cc4-73f851c9b943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3\\,\\text{mm/s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269722} {:answer/id #uuid "1709d32a-fd2c-5cdf-9c84-65f745613c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\,\\text{mm/s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269723} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2a61db-d5c3-5cb8-8be0-35ec62010629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3\\,\\text{mm/s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269724} {:answer/id #uuid "67ac2236-bdab-5016-934c-5487a67c75ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{mm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269725} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd59c3-0f80-5511-8daf-3564d39d4c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3\\,\\text{mm/s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ac2236-bdab-5016-934c-5487a67c75ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{mm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269725} :answer-field/id #uuid "2504befa-4a60-5b6d-991c-159ad262ed76" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80fa2c46-8e7b-5923-bd32-30e6745dadc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109020" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in millimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=9t-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =9-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =9-3(2)^{2} \\\\  & =9-12 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-3|=3\\,\\text{mm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7178be99-8cd0-53db-ab2b-1a4bd54d5038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269806} {:answer/id #uuid "e305494a-f98d-56b6-91ec-47916f3c4c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269807} {:answer/id #uuid "97724f34-ae96-5ef1-9428-df01ea1e1772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269808} {:answer/id #uuid "1c896760-dc01-50c5-8e8b-4b0d424e6105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7178be99-8cd0-53db-ab2b-1a4bd54d5038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269806} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea7ad72-0e3c-529e-8276-508a352ebed4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbf7b752-cbd4-57de-8347-862618b357a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-110798" :question/problem "Solve ${log}_{2}(x-2)={log}_{2}(2x-3)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & ={log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ x-2 & =2x-3 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(1-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2\\cdot 1-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, ${log}_{2}(-1)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d7e182-b298-5f94-a314-a10b321bf8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269810} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f44493-de44-5faa-88f8-7da0deb55732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-2,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "0e152e1c-49fa-5f3f-8b76-4d8b625cbcd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} {:answer/id #uuid "117f14a6-a963-5d5c-a450-a45c7d8fe2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+3,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d7a8c7-a5d5-5b11-a77e-2f92af341958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3de9cf-84d9-5ffb-a26d-3666f3702b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-110799" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d9b7624-8b63-5c92-9e87-99970e683bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256874} {:answer/id #uuid "30a05358-5f44-575b-a9de-ce1822364fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256875} {:answer/id #uuid "930f846a-0de4-5297-bf95-95ab3ae4dead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256876} {:answer/id #uuid "a56ef72d-5597-55cb-9d06-627f7b68b3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256877} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaf444c-54a3-5b3a-aced-cf563009d2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256878} {:answer/id #uuid "a78c54fe-b2e5-5eb5-83ac-6e9175fcf819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256879} {:answer/id #uuid "19b0761b-be8e-589d-b2c9-ce99c5c71159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256880} {:answer/id #uuid "dec88327-3398-521e-bf43-d73f8b93ee45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b7624-8b63-5c92-9e87-99970e683bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256874} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6e0b01-1512-5734-99de-0dd680458bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8190dc0-5e3e-595b-8144-fb26d1f0ac64" :question/math-academy-id "q-112591" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff00665-7ba7-57a9-9200-7a3014f15076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257213} {:answer/id #uuid "64dd75a1-90f3-5ad7-8e60-4e14e84283db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257214} {:answer/id #uuid "cad6966a-4554-523d-89eb-dce58e73f94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257215} {:answer/id #uuid "05093784-be42-5759-bb5b-604a75a80dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257216} {:answer/id #uuid "82e7bbd8-4fef-5055-a846-e47b2a0ffed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257217} {:answer/id #uuid "04ac6c12-ac73-51cc-a51b-a8742a4800c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257218} {:answer/id #uuid "bc151bf3-4c5c-5a81-aa51-90e66c0d7986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257219} {:answer/id #uuid "f40ab244-9e72-55f0-b52a-d771ac9a7696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff00665-7ba7-57a9-9200-7a3014f15076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257213} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18db54b-ee1a-5fdc-8fe9-ce6240a0127c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257212}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aa45b23-9bad-5043-be22-ae4d5bfb0139" :question/math-academy-id "q-117331" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$\nII. - $-\\frac{3\\sqrt{e}}{\\pi }$\nIII. - $-17\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9401dc4c-13f3-52a6-b78d-e36edf3146d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257223} {:answer/id #uuid "a1db6f03-cfcf-5c11-a6e0-fb9d3bc0980e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257224} {:answer/id #uuid "dd174e53-0b09-57ca-9db2-48079e2d2b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "695fe791-b8c2-529f-bd48-86e408e634fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257226} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a96266-1e97-5508-9b0e-56ea0b1de739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257227} {:answer/id #uuid "252ec23b-ce4f-5c47-921c-ef7e64c323a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257228} {:answer/id #uuid "fd553f76-6c99-5537-b8d7-b6e36bb5e883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257229} {:answer/id #uuid "12d7c230-fbe4-5468-aa94-253457d2cee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9401dc4c-13f3-52a6-b78d-e36edf3146d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257223} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d1385a2-b018-503a-aad0-bcabd7d5444b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb24cccb-acc4-53dc-9162-57e55441c4d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117332" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $-\\sqrt{10}$\nII. - $-\\frac{e}{5}$\nIII. - $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee1ecf7-6286-5bde-bd10-8f63518c2501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270287} {:answer/id #uuid "6db865b2-f956-5683-9301-5af3ec17f296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270288} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d7d71b-df20-5639-b3de-8a3399c7a2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270289} {:answer/id #uuid "990432d5-aa9e-55a1-86a4-38bbdb83257a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270290} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9d67a0-7df8-5724-886c-83fe8e3d55bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e9d67a0-7df8-5724-886c-83fe8e3d55bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270291} :answer-field/id #uuid "da1b19a3-bb34-5658-96bb-9cb2bea5ef4e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bc8c542-2e1c-5460-9d76-b1be87bfdf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-117401" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca22daee-b942-5426-ab0f-e71349302254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270992} {:answer/id #uuid "18e36a19-28db-550a-b839-d9b5ee0e5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270993} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9c9fc1-02b2-5585-83ba-8b69e57a8206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270994} {:answer/id #uuid "5d420ab0-47a6-5cc4-8290-392aea770550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270995} {:answer/id #uuid "33470e5f-b794-5f0a-abf4-00193b692921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d420ab0-47a6-5cc4-8290-392aea770550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270995} :answer-field/id #uuid "adcb0448-2f53-5b30-b30a-68919c5b5815" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b428fa76-e87c-5e0d-99bc-e0b26ffcdc6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14181" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges." :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Monotonic Polynomial and Rational Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d7b8dc-c539-5a30-b787-d5cb6fbad3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258216} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1148d6-c098-5ca6-80f2-f7f770795e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258217} {:answer/id #uuid "650b0d5b-3ebf-5077-9ac9-272237398d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258218} {:answer/id #uuid "5434b004-2468-5add-bf08-b34db24725d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258219} {:answer/id #uuid "46879293-9588-5332-9d71-9bc89252aa2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258220} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac94f30-91f1-58ff-9677-bb0ee6545524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258221} {:answer/id #uuid "ef47b9b8-b919-5c03-a9a0-ad6ea6f59897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258222} {:answer/id #uuid "df5dd82f-cd2e-5351-bdeb-d1a91d62486a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46879293-9588-5332-9d71-9bc89252aa2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258220} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf614fb-085c-5189-ba08-b842d326e7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258215}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b089f948-9e77-5fe2-b184-bf4a5b05b38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143201" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8}{n+4}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8}{n+4}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{n+5})}{(\\frac{8}{n+4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(n+4)}{8(n+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{n+4}{n+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{n+4}{n+5}<1,\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0<L<1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3eae4ea-fb38-5efa-843e-783327b82702" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f834033-2b8b-5fa7-a3da-76a019b14f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271230} {:answer/id #uuid "721519b0-5023-5a2a-bb1d-ad8a904b79ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271231} {:answer/id #uuid "b874668e-cea1-560b-9bd0-2f05bedbc16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271232} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aeb2b6-3313-5e7c-968e-70e7e452154b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271233} {:answer/id #uuid "693f34ef-66fb-5c20-addc-e8e69cb39eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b874668e-cea1-560b-9bd0-2f05bedbc16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271232} :answer-field/id #uuid "25df2076-f538-5c9f-8d37-8de2abb05827" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa319499-3756-5d68-bea5-48fa20a410aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-17197" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}(\\sqrt{x+2}+1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}+1.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\sqrt{-2+2}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+1. \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d0e6532c8b96d7a3ab85d73a145a79292b8b0101cfa46ebffb08aefdeb06687b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that for the left-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a95ad0f7-1041-5a05-93ed-e9dafdc08db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − e^{5})/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271340} {:answer/id #uuid "562d7875-47e8-5f2f-8d29-13772f8b1f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271341} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4fbcd3-e6f8-5233-893e-ccecca02a429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5 − e^{2})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271342} {:answer/id #uuid "c40f0a76-42fc-561f-b506-36e9776aa4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − e^{2})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271343} {:answer/id #uuid "f1988905-9a1c-5b05-b1ac-efe63d492d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5 − e^{5})/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "562d7875-47e8-5f2f-8d29-13772f8b1f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271341} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68a56fd-9dae-516a-87a3-2201c59392c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274546}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95caf8a3-14ba-5322-a4f2-b737554e209c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19181" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323b3755-ec7c-5fcc-b76e-ad324f1c092e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271360} {:answer/id #uuid "1c671bfd-0ecf-5f9a-97ea-faeea92c74ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271361} {:answer/id #uuid "cba24aa0-d112-5a04-9d47-dcac9873aa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271362} {:answer/id #uuid "92270710-ac05-5656-abff-e1b0aacfe956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271363} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd0684b-4b10-5516-b6b7-666e6a1bfc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba24aa0-d112-5a04-9d47-dcac9873aa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271362} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f80d69-12eb-5fbb-b094-33d9346ff56e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274550}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75b4db1-5076-5b68-9e0a-66e78c347afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19452" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}t+5\\operatorname{cos}t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}t+5\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}t-5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-10\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-5\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-10{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds is $-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "630b2520-5350-5957-b7a9-af9bb2527748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271493} {:answer/id #uuid "53a87d29-28b8-566f-b916-d63b6444d582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271494} {:answer/id #uuid "87140a10-f79c-5163-8b5b-10fbf1a63735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271495} {:answer/id #uuid "7c84ab5a-9176-5b3b-8608-5e6961f32830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271496} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8243fa-3b33-53f3-9e41-fc66d4d82a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c84ab5a-9176-5b3b-8608-5e6961f32830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271496} :answer-field/id #uuid "de95a2bb-350d-5138-8bbc-d8e038e0cb49" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274576}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a37d360-2bb9-53ba-8a4f-830d6a3e6ba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-2358" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=6{t}^{3}-19{t}^{2}+13,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}-19{t}^{2}+13) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-38t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(18{t}^{2}-38t) \\\\  & =36t-38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=70,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & =36t-38 \\\\ 36t & =108 \\\\ t & =3\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time when the acceleration is $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $3$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d772ade-e9c4-53d9-b060-df7fa35e5dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271547} {:answer/id #uuid "8b19c853-2e91-5327-ac54-702cd3b9e959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271548} {:answer/id #uuid "96d1b195-0122-5283-bae5-cca01b7d0678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271549} {:answer/id #uuid "ff92b997-987f-5457-8118-fe843ce149e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271550} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b593c-be38-5819-b5c1-25891fe717e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff92b997-987f-5457-8118-fe843ce149e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271550} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dede46c-069f-5fd6-8e9b-89d8dcbc6afa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "154a3540-0e80-5977-9f93-8017dcb4011f" :question/math-academy-id "q-24901" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd87916d-9a14-5874-95b9-fd8ebb17b328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271607} {:answer/id #uuid "14d8a114-0c6f-5b55-94a4-33049f7fd1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271608} {:answer/id #uuid "41c43cd2-cbff-5951-82db-dffe46aa7697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271609} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2da6a0-42d5-53cb-99ca-39fe98091af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271610} {:answer/id #uuid "849271c1-a2cb-5c8a-964f-3ea7f87fd043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c43cd2-cbff-5951-82db-dffe46aa7697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271609} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbded57-dfc7-587e-81f6-143aca803f14" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274599}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f84765eb-bbc5-55fb-8f02-81e4b4814923" :question/math-academy-id "q-26531" :question/problem "If ${log}_{3}(6-2x)=4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})$, then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}({(\\sqrt{3})}^{4}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\ 6-2x & =9 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(6-2(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(6+3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})^{4} \\\\  & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6e7e60-4fe8-5e35-b73d-dbdcdc3459cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271612} {:answer/id #uuid "ed42fe13-a182-5a58-8aed-70548aa24d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271613} {:answer/id #uuid "e3974706-c909-5446-8a50-629f8b2fae4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271614} {:answer/id #uuid "8549b760-529f-5713-a214-53d93f343d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271615} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf0fbe9-3cbf-5cc8-8838-39c12d9fad3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8549b760-529f-5713-a214-53d93f343d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271615} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5450e4-f7c4-5427-971f-fc00391bdb91" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274600}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "154ed866-22c4-5f95-b448-f473ba0b174a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26532" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{2}(3x+1)={log}_{2}(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}(4) & ={log}_{2}(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8a2cc0-3064-527c-aa4a-d38a220c6751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8a2cc0-3064-527c-aa4a-d38a220c6751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259356} :answer-field/id #uuid "721c43ef-685e-5f93-8853-6882618ceaad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259355}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6ef0baa-19f2-5c87-8441-104458b5100c" :question/math-academy-id "q-300756" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-8$ causes $y=-8{x}^{2}$ to become a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1d6244-8637-5d3b-b306-c756db7494b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1d6244-8637-5d3b-b306-c756db7494b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259359} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0467a37-cde6-50ac-8248-b8ad329354c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259358}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f376207d-e128-5c61-ab06-8cda85b3c269" :question/math-academy-id "q-300767" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7d113b2-2b28-5f24-ba8b-62b4f9edb7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7d113b2-2b28-5f24-ba8b-62b4f9edb7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259365} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac13cb1-f847-5dfd-b77f-8b30d856c849" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dd5962e-7f16-511b-9886-c7af08a85022" :question/math-academy-id "q-302050" :question/problem "If $p(x)=q(x)=-2x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{4}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(-2x)}^{4}\\cdot {(-2x)}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(16{x}^{4}\\cdot -8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((16\\cdot -8){x}^{4+3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-128\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-128$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-128{x}^{7})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f158ff-5efa-5a7b-be91-b288a1bd7ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f158ff-5efa-5a7b-be91-b288a1bd7ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259368} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a167e5e-f8ff-5323-922c-31faa60e4d63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259367}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03fac39f-7bd4-593c-8143-5d6d29276bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-302152" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{6}(4x-2)$ is well defined for $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}(4\\cdot 2-2)}{2+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}({6}^{1})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d107785c-82d2-5dbf-9293-e66fad67ba54" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Sided Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229701dc-ff3f-5fa5-a496-0eff3b7db0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229701dc-ff3f-5fa5-a496-0eff3b7db0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259371} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee443d81-2272-555f-9267-d4c6d32ea69b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259370}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a119145-cfea-5bc3-a23b-0c4ed68fe966" :question/math-academy-id "q-302174" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(-4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bb8d326b427d481c69ad021a831efadf77cb90540dd79d0c3edd29f68cae41ec.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3eae4ea-fb38-5efa-843e-783327b82702" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ee5394-b2dd-50d9-b7b0-77c1f5409d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8ee5394-b2dd-50d9-b7b0-77c1f5409d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259380} :answer-field/id #uuid "656f0b5e-8a20-5b17-b807-c16226a1ba77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259379}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf9923c2-2a75-5d31-b240-73c84287294f" :question/math-academy-id "q-302976" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we know that the limit of $\\sqrt{5x-m}$ exists, we can substitute $x=2$ into this expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m} & =\\sqrt{5(2)-m} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $\\sqrt{5},$ we can equate the above with $\\sqrt{5},$ and then solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-m} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10-m & =5 \\\\ m & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259570} :answer-field/id #uuid "1afda0f2-c499-5fb4-a591-efc3a186f139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259569}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1e3d3bc-82fc-5fa2-90ce-eb77683f685b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320665" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d056fb6f-7f25-5012-83fb-2a87bb1466d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d056fb6f-7f25-5012-83fb-2a87bb1466d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259573} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ffd3b3-5c41-5076-8346-8b9088e2acb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fadd708b-85a8-59ac-a2d8-87b9003aff34" :question/math-academy-id "q-320723" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ac59b0-6e4f-5dce-ae60-9b5f04dd2348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04ac59b0-6e4f-5dce-ae60-9b5f04dd2348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259579} :answer-field/id #uuid "3181f7fa-572e-51e1-934e-8f5364b3af9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259578}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "100a789f-1084-51be-ba86-3a2a27edfbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321138" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4fbdff-9f6e-56e1-a6c8-7bcfab6bbe8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c4fbdff-9f6e-56e1-a6c8-7bcfab6bbe8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259741} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b5cebc-ed49-5730-9a88-a8e0f04c3d34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0de860f6-11f7-5006-bbaa-d1ca0fb6d2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334171" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5268fcad-4ae8-573e-80fe-07a9521ab94c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5268fcad-4ae8-573e-80fe-07a9521ab94c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259744} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd90552-824a-5df9-b4cd-4c041b6d04fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259743}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c04f322-6297-5a59-99f0-946aa65e8f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-334189" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6bb6be2-87df-5a90-b8da-3fd783476439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bb6be2-87df-5a90-b8da-3fd783476439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259747} :answer-field/id #uuid "b713fe42-1112-55c0-a3e9-179614c29a7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "521e4421-8c12-5483-a9ad-eb3663c791eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/26e2e4a51d40e9ed8e6a8fab342935e944e750587534c7cedc1855cbfddfbfc5.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}+5x-6$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2a8fcf-b952-5bc9-8904-cca87e7400a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f2a8fcf-b952-5bc9-8904-cca87e7400a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259750} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d91666-8f76-51fe-bd30-9969a887fd2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bd36deb-148b-5a1a-b1d2-7ce3ec169a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334245" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+4}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $4$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/77080e0c2085e5678929e33f8e0588a3639bfc11b3b629dc63310acbeba6ab57.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+4}=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e7bcfd-aecd-5b2b-9943-1b6c5632144f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56e7bcfd-aecd-5b2b-9943-1b6c5632144f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259753} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aae44f7-4f87-50b6-95ad-97f979d6701c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259752}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17706661-995f-5eb6-aa6e-cb6de0a1007a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334267" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3231fdde89ea829347de177152ee793203612d5cffd074e0b2738389a2581f17.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4500fac8-af20-5c20-911d-1f32dbb57955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4500fac8-af20-5c20-911d-1f32dbb57955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259759} :answer-field/id #uuid "335c7175-1070-581d-8e1f-409546a66e39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259758}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e386cce-90e7-5313-a0f9-874041a6503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336160" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t,\\,0<t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400aa6d7-ad0b-55c5-9730-4139ca77b867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "400aa6d7-ad0b-55c5-9730-4139ca77b867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259762} :answer-field/id #uuid "d79b4b85-5bf6-5426-8987-1d99cefbf281" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259761}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1f7c427-fd09-51c4-aeca-22ae40dad476" :question/math-academy-id "q-336189" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t+8\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+8\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6(0)+8(-1) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-8|=8\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d1a1b1-aaed-55d9-93ee-6e710a836e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d1a1b1-aaed-55d9-93ee-6e710a836e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259768} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbbe1e2b-3bac-52e4-9a4d-e5eeb164f79a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2eb4629d-fc7e-5d92-9fc1-95bd60f68d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-336433" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by\n$v(t)=\\frac{t+3}{t+1}.$\nCalculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhen $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t+3}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot (t+1)-(t+3)\\cdot (1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+1-t-3}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ (t+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ t+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ t & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=1$ since $t\\ge 0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{1+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26810715-9796-5c64-9514-da8abe94bd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26810715-9796-5c64-9514-da8abe94bd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259771} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e67a12c-5589-5fb2-9462-3a40855293a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c2973b7-9660-53ce-9738-4f12653a5fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336896" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $\\frac{-5}{2}$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb907c0d-3b8a-5e38-bd9a-bee10c75c1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb907c0d-3b8a-5e38-bd9a-bee10c75c1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259780} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90da28f-f2df-5f89-bc5c-259715754e58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259779}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39ff6f11-126f-5d17-90bb-5fef510de3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336955" :question/problem "If $f(x)=3{x}^{9}$ and $g(x)=-2{x}^{-11}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{9})=3{x}^{11}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ and $g(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{11}\\cdot (-2{x}^{-11})) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad10ca6b-d130-559b-a201-15127659de13" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity of a Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b834336-f44e-5ced-a4b6-82897769dbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b834336-f44e-5ced-a4b6-82897769dbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259786} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2d9220-d1e0-536d-a6a4-5038fe9720c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff3ed87e-89ed-5503-bbd2-d542dc726a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-337875" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{-(x-3)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{-(x-3)}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3fb10f30533c2d1390085223e1ea78ea7d23e723bbea0550d7d236368adcfa82.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631dd0e-a893-5099-8b52-e7a543e7f783" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Cube Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d50905c-b55c-5093-a351-7a4a8afb1004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d50905c-b55c-5093-a351-7a4a8afb1004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259789} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc19a03-5ebe-5d9b-8e53-685a0cef23f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259788}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eed63dc5-4938-533a-9ddd-3ca6174a3bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-337889" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2) & =\\sqrt[3]{8(1-1)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0}+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f945ef94-171c-5c19-adb3-f496408dc31b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit at Infinity of a Cube Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa7aed1f-10c7-5561-a53c-b78f7a4b7584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa7aed1f-10c7-5561-a53c-b78f7a4b7584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259792} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9471b6-a3f3-5184-a0af-2583d9703a7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3270dc4-14ef-5cdb-84a6-80872a5da97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337898" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{11-7x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{11-7x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/63ea04a354807ffcd7a6b772b78f9208d35a30252ee79539f8b49ac39e02da7e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f945ef94-171c-5c19-adb3-f496408dc31b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit at Infinity of a Cube Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e9e953-973a-5e44-8737-3fb8a6082dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e9e953-973a-5e44-8737-3fb8a6082dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259795} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce0a9de-e9e7-5aa6-9164-a965d01e9c91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259794}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82ca8780-792e-5ee3-91d0-231128bd3704" :question/math-academy-id "q-337907" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{5x-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1e154aca18a2f40e9076e617b15042c13739f87f9e26e12259c7d1d71f70aef3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578b2cf5-183e-5a1f-b8c5-5dba90fd4584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578b2cf5-183e-5a1f-b8c5-5dba90fd4584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259828} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8ec972-c67d-5fea-a1b3-1aac198c5c4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d11fc22e-27dc-5e16-bb71-552dd8da0121" :question/math-academy-id "q-338671" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3(x+2)}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea78c6f0-f62c-57c9-8f0a-9bae1cffea30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea78c6f0-f62c-57c9-8f0a-9bae1cffea30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259831} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be028ea-240c-5f99-b73d-942fa2a9e307" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259830}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae096ba8-7305-5c9b-9b5b-b942d8e27c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338679" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4ec5d3-d8f6-557d-baf7-74248b61e78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e4ec5d3-d8f6-557d-baf7-74248b61e78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854} :answer-field/id #uuid "b52ad5f2-2eaf-5cc7-b3fc-39152ab8f994" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2bb04bd-6dcb-51fa-87c5-f2822ddf4961" :question/math-academy-id "q-339827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)$ is well defined for $x>-7$ and $x<5,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x) & ={log}_{3}(2+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-2) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9)\\cdot {log}_{3}(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}({3}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0ecdff-bf89-5b51-9959-e731f64dd826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ecdff-bf89-5b51-9959-e731f64dd826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ff9c97-e39b-5817-82bb-d0588b34b4c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef5eb0fa-9598-5322-bdda-f9890c3d2532" :question/math-academy-id "q-339846" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(-6x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8a4a1bc79f9d096c3ebe2b4ff37e8682f74ab04583101dfe1d7f1d5f2e8df8c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46387cb9-77c6-5309-9165-30b1b22f3401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46387cb9-77c6-5309-9165-30b1b22f3401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259863} :answer-field/id #uuid "260b2018-b639-5013-ac54-7fe6b71f58fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb759e9-9584-5702-9892-b88d8e48dfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eb759e9-9584-5702-9892-b88d8e48dfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259865} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e11637-23e5-564b-a2cf-551237109653" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259864}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4bb0f1e-ed08-55ef-acdf-b88dc863724a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339964" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=1$ from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+3x-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}[(x-2)^{2}+3x-5] \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+3(1)-5 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+3-5 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4))+2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d107785c-82d2-5dbf-9293-e66fad67ba54" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Sided Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f7824ed-d5d8-5046-89e2-611be36b1699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7824ed-d5d8-5046-89e2-611be36b1699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259883} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e77ef3b-fe07-5d48-950d-dd2eb6ff6cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259882}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4b20c1c-6829-532a-8642-48abc149b75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340087" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(9x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1ead64d0b64a005122fa34c65677d5e7980c2a76245bbaca572325b8681ae958.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7528b06a-a2d6-5ebb-bfb3-2d4a4bd9e29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7528b06a-a2d6-5ebb-bfb3-2d4a4bd9e29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259892} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fec6cb4-e826-5dbe-bb32-219c987b8a6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddd0688-dbb1-5edb-8d08-525b26a06be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340307" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}c-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}c-x \\\\  & =c-(-1) \\\\  & =c+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =1+2-1 \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c+1 & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68cf9dd0-e510-5126-b4d0-c090d47eb468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac9{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68cf9dd0-e510-5126-b4d0-c090d47eb468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac9{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260076} :answer-field/id #uuid "d850b06c-59bd-5254-9a07-b72bfab72cfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6de2ae0-e267-50e2-aafb-3b65201ff387" :question/math-academy-id "q-347375" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{27}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{27}{125})}{(\\frac{9}{25})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8a9c045-18f6-58c4-9746-9a119ed6975c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f822b0c-bbbe-5841-a487-068d8891fd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f822b0c-bbbe-5841-a487-068d8891fd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260097} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f615646-a0cf-5571-a02a-aaabfb151da4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260096}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "752cdffe-91c8-55b4-9c94-2e01e1ed83a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-349060" :question/problem "Find the eighth partial sum ${s}_{8}$ of the series  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n+3).$\n\n${s}_{8}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4n+3.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)+3=7 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)+3=11 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)+3=15,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4$ and first term ${a}_{1}=7.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{8}=4(8)+3=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{8}=\\frac{8}{2}(7+35)=168.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5d98fb1-73c0-52a8-aa21-b27565c0b3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d98fb1-73c0-52a8-aa21-b27565c0b3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260159} :answer-field/id #uuid "146a7171-bd46-5961-a19f-d6ee20c17aea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b90e1c4-d521-5585-b6db-776d9a11ab9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352279" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n}$ given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=4.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factor outside of the summation sign gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n} & =9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & =9(4) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a115faf-45a8-5cc1-8450-dccb5431ab58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271904} {:answer/id #uuid "72926a43-c53a-55de-87f1-92c3d8e06b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271905} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c43e1c-755b-575d-bb12-b84230249d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271906} {:answer/id #uuid "e319acc5-8d59-5dfc-9795-340d384671f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271907} {:answer/id #uuid "cc99a8cc-bd33-58ab-85aa-1a0d03b3f3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc99a8cc-bd33-58ab-85aa-1a0d03b3f3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271908} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc8b77e-8df7-5a26-8a90-3605454571c2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bafbcb3a-c207-54ff-a32e-667f79749c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-35615" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{-2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{-2})}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{8}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a7dd7e-f1ab-5f73-9100-4947f6932adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271919} {:answer/id #uuid "22309c28-a80b-50d5-b54f-d4a0c35d1374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271920} {:answer/id #uuid "bde1ec42-fc79-550b-98af-3bd016cdb616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271921} {:answer/id #uuid "9f103186-f24e-5a2a-8baf-d99dc867b054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271922} {:answer/id #uuid "92df029e-138d-502c-bb57-349a6a74cf71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22309c28-a80b-50d5-b54f-d4a0c35d1374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271920} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a2ba3e-97c1-5860-a456-1fedddfac777" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "090d4d7a-51e4-5340-8ffd-149afaef8dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-36077" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{2}{x}^{2}+6x-3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{2}{x}^{2}+6x-3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\,(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d07f305-c5cf-5446-8b9b-b8b71455b709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271934} {:answer/id #uuid "828a3b3a-8f9b-57f9-bc24-4866772614c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271935} {:answer/id #uuid "d2841e07-2c4f-5ed9-8178-5a353673279e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271936} {:answer/id #uuid "3a152977-91a1-58d5-a549-0c0277e77e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271937} {:answer/id #uuid "31f0eecd-adf4-5c7d-b953-e37423e6515f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2841e07-2c4f-5ed9-8178-5a353673279e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271936} :answer-field/id #uuid "2362752a-dede-5607-adfc-614171fb8267" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274666}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45bcd46e-63f8-54ff-8722-e835994d8ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-36722" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=-2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={2}^{x}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-2)}^{-}}({2}^{x}-3) \\\\  & ={2}^{-2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-2)}^{+}}\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdae2cee-0a56-54e7-9eb8-4d809f0cd339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271939} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b5bba5-27b5-559a-8cb1-861d3da1d65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271940} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fa3279-39b7-53b5-9ab8-c1228a33756e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271941} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3b6a52-1a9b-511e-9324-899b176586dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271942} {:answer/id #uuid "2eea1261-1377-5c37-9170-b39cea28c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5fa3279-39b7-53b5-9ab8-c1228a33756e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271941} :answer-field/id #uuid "55137a29-065e-560b-8016-0574440e0fb7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58c75b63-39e4-5a53-bb97-bce7efb5c37c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36735" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=1$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}-x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}-x+2) \\\\  & =-(1)^{2}-1+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d69d888-12d2-5395-85eb-c8a808ccd48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(3)+2$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271949} {:answer/id #uuid "2207b64f-b627-52fa-82ea-d25435518ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(3)-1$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271950} {:answer/id #uuid "687bdcc8-9615-5bea-9257-aaba5ca16548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $3$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271951} {:answer/id #uuid "158a1d18-7a14-5439-9cbb-1a8e6c826b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(3)+1$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271952} {:answer/id #uuid "76999989-c8de-55c4-ae6f-f62689c48a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(3)-2$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687bdcc8-9615-5bea-9257-aaba5ca16548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $3$; c = $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271951} :answer-field/id #uuid "62fef830-b433-5626-ae3a-d54cfa3a0fd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274669}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03dad123-1833-5311-a4f2-ae8087879f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-36744" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+cx+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x+b) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-3(2)+b \\\\  & =4-6+b \\\\  & =b-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ b-2 & =1 \\\\ b & =1+2 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+cx+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+cx+1) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2}+c(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+2c+1 \\\\  & =2c-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2c-3 & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1+3}{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e151c787-e6d6-5110-909b-76e716bd6a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271994} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c3d8e8-35e7-516c-ad72-56a50d443b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271995} {:answer/id #uuid "8e251da1-2731-576b-b954-003f09a5b2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271996} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1a738d-378c-5a94-a311-04321297cf28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271997} {:answer/id #uuid "21ba7086-3e50-5b54-ad19-e4af56f1ab60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e151c787-e6d6-5110-909b-76e716bd6a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271994} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d87a6b0-3e31-5d44-85fd-69d4d1c0d282" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7945d8b0-07f1-5fd2-9eec-daaae659ced4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38763" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2a8352e83cdbdbfc250125d9d1e0f36ee079ca2793973cfde6eaacc2943fe8d3.png)\n\nWhat could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "796d245f-ca1f-57f0-9e17-a1cf04421e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3((x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271999} {:answer/id #uuid "79006a6b-bd92-5860-a24d-28a16c9020e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}((x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272000} {:answer/id #uuid "2728588c-9e27-5f1f-9528-9f123dbd04b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272001} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea563d-466c-518b-a466-3536ec974167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2((x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272002} {:answer/id #uuid "75c3fc3a-ffa4-5c18-94ac-4a7a3ed7902b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-((x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2728588c-9e27-5f1f-9528-9f123dbd04b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d2c4b7-4c14-58c3-9abc-866d754a970d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9585562-3c89-5dba-a5e8-5e2ad82590cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-38765" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8ec1115a3f38481a3013797b2824a0a13148b2bb0e7138964966b873094727c1.png)\n\nWhat could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely four distinct roots, $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+1)^{2}(x-1)(x-2)(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03c91ac6-d580-536d-90a8-dc4bebdcdf7b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3b7034-9e01-5146-a3e9-4009c0b3aa50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272059} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d240c6-c5d0-5072-837a-81ccaa00db32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272060} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42c06d-73ab-57d6-9e7d-bd88f388dd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272061} {:answer/id #uuid "c049e5a3-bf8f-584d-a878-514de61d4fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272062} {:answer/id #uuid "500a6cf7-bdae-5317-832b-d398961d2351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c049e5a3-bf8f-584d-a878-514de61d4fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272062} :answer-field/id #uuid "13309ac9-1eef-5855-b820-ebc7323d03a8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b2b439e-829e-542e-9779-42c02c781e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40655" :question/problem "Find the domain of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{6}(3x+4)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y={log}_{6}x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $3x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n3x+4>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & >0 \\\\ 3x & >-4 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\frac{4}{3},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03c91ac6-d580-536d-90a8-dc4bebdcdf7b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b487e6-5c47-5bfd-9e16-6dcfd5e3130a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074} {:answer/id #uuid "f03647a0-e134-549d-bcb1-38e86085d534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272075} {:answer/id #uuid "1498e8c3-9a6f-5642-9090-e6b4ff454757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d19f4-6d55-5d3c-aa73-713a5148dfec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077} {:answer/id #uuid "669d8902-ac99-565f-9c78-fe886de21f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1498e8c3-9a6f-5642-9090-e6b4ff454757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076} :answer-field/id #uuid "94e929dd-f700-56a3-a215-b107919e1865" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f0af432-165a-5fe2-b10e-a843efae2448" :question/math-academy-id "q-4115" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{x}{2}-1)-4.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{log}x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $\\frac{x}{2}-1$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & >1 \\\\ x & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1587e39e-c7bf-5385-b726-a1b497f7c772" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b98bb2-f766-5129-90fa-692e70687aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272112} {:answer/id #uuid "038da50e-141e-541a-ac01-0f3f0246df66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272113} {:answer/id #uuid "8f580fce-975c-596a-a3a6-2ab074f09a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8ac670-76ab-5bee-a0ea-98c3ac2e5df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272115} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8db2c6-e6b6-53ff-afad-0365829f041a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "038da50e-141e-541a-ac01-0f3f0246df66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272113} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b120ea7-9602-5a2d-9f1d-37747ff0fcba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf2dc9f7-7d68-5872-92d4-9358e4fec6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-42443" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is not in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70721b98-2f05-5e66-bd72-db0f92b93be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272206} {:answer/id #uuid "0126a508-3b34-5c20-88f5-249887e043e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272207} {:answer/id #uuid "fab89a04-460d-522f-8ddf-a478e9390d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272208} {:answer/id #uuid "92faae70-3db9-536c-a713-a81a7fb3b7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272209} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc0955-b07c-5998-8fb3-2c3514b4f963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc0955-b07c-5998-8fb3-2c3514b4f963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272210} :answer-field/id #uuid "4190935f-2850-5da8-9fe2-b93f28458bba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b240cab-24fa-5335-a943-6cdfe8310c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-46975" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eef9ae-38aa-5703-aac3-2451e33a8270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8a9d6b-0979-5fb9-b76b-2776f7d86fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272301} {:answer/id #uuid "a571305f-d599-562f-859e-f54a0ced4e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272302} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cde92a-a19b-51c4-9ede-d847ccfb12be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272303} {:answer/id #uuid "e296361e-1bae-541e-8892-e3669c21a6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81eef9ae-38aa-5703-aac3-2451e33a8270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300} :answer-field/id #uuid "96024b78-3b4a-5982-ba12-399d21c62cb2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3187ea03-2a2b-58df-8eac-de1e0d9676e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48208" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7).$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7)=0+7=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5414e634-9b33-5dac-9686-4e570efbc7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae30257-b2ed-5530-aa1a-81c68f18f215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272315} {:answer/id #uuid "29485322-2787-540d-be84-067a8f7f9bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272316} {:answer/id #uuid "f13b3bf4-dccd-5daa-a57d-07ec1c2482f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272317} {:answer/id #uuid "68d51223-a31b-52a8-a9ad-97bb285efa75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272318} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9a60c3-adc3-5508-b4c1-a17131c7f32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9a60c3-adc3-5508-b4c1-a17131c7f32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272319} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f0fba1-cf1e-5d42-be20-a049cb499444" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274743}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c4a4a0b-e1a5-5f41-82c8-6f7ce8f75158" :question/math-academy-id "q-48221" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\nx{e}^{-2x}=\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4bf4c40-5b93-5922-a11a-624e6a563264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $1/e^2$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272355} {:answer/id #uuid "0f55770d-2c5d-5c20-924a-525334c33dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $(1/e^2)-2$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272356} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1826b0-60f4-5972-a6c0-f2020ddcae08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $(1/e^2)-1$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272357} {:answer/id #uuid "7156ad08-c531-5b37-8ce7-804988d4e5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $(1/e^2)+1$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272358} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf89456-678b-529f-b873-14ed7d337638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $(1/e^2)+2$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4bf4c40-5b93-5922-a11a-624e6a563264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial position m = $1/e^2$; initial velocity m per s = $3/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272355} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e9d6af-3a4e-5bc1-9e46-d3241745c237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274751}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c048a62-43d8-5898-82bc-9cc14066f325" :question/math-academy-id "q-48705" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)={e}^{3t-2}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds.  Find the position and velocity of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)={e}^{3t-2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3(0)-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4739f80c-281e-57b1-b089-f7008f9a34af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272370} {:answer/id #uuid "2d031088-204d-57ca-8df1-5668a7d7b452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272371} {:answer/id #uuid "52bde355-7c40-5dd8-bd00-8c7739cb8b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272372} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd91e7-5e8f-561a-8bc2-5201b02b8351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272373} {:answer/id #uuid "68384735-f09f-5a7d-81fe-4b553dee7882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd91e7-5e8f-561a-8bc2-5201b02b8351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272373} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ad0ff7-d16f-5c1f-888e-2eb1656355a6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72cf5a4e-ff4b-5bf3-a1c6-4b089563c4f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-48866" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $3{x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/80dcb33426c9981efdb4eddb4bbd33e75aeb5267cd776acfbda99758e010128a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7ee8a3f-e6a3-59ab-b8de-6c8706f4f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272375} {:answer/id #uuid "7df6baba-4feb-5e4c-aff7-27e3972d7861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272376} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5a6f12-4c32-58e6-9e6b-8062feb109e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272377} {:answer/id #uuid "9705875d-1c97-5e98-8476-38eb8bb31066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272378} {:answer/id #uuid "b383c100-2292-51f2-af66-aa821354e207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9705875d-1c97-5e98-8476-38eb8bb31066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272378} :answer-field/id #uuid "805c7d26-afc1-5e59-9989-baddc916c56c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6e8da81-1916-527c-8075-091e80f07bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48869" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n3\\le \\operatorname{cos}x+4\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2c4c732472ffcfa4a42e7a0dd7e1ff55a9b4d2f0d5b0b652b875efa284d9274d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2fe707c-9edf-558f-baa7-5d4b0795b7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272545} {:answer/id #uuid "aa41f134-2c08-57e0-b677-8eac274b5dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272546} {:answer/id #uuid "071bb958-8da2-5354-a92b-57261a0c2fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272547} {:answer/id #uuid "92159337-5a56-5bc7-8b19-8dd1c1f7b0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272548} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc2b540-4f0a-5902-9821-fcf45996a729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "071bb958-8da2-5354-a92b-57261a0c2fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272547} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7ef45b-505e-50a2-8cea-53a9bafcb33b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc5e4f48-0994-54a5-be66-79482fad2c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-54449" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cb507d2c8846ff1a55d2ff32c2db9264be34c008fff61098a3e3ad81d8c1c4de.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}(2x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6ee6772-332f-539b-a2d9-2032dd9180c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272933} {:answer/id #uuid "576d4ff7-3b84-5720-a6cb-20f1fbbddfb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272934} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc73844-f240-5694-bbe3-2c14ca08a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272935} {:answer/id #uuid "0a0bdd21-5c89-5a39-b5a8-3a339896e13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272936} {:answer/id #uuid "04cd7af2-250e-5e79-8e3e-a9b7bcab022e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04cd7af2-250e-5e79-8e3e-a9b7bcab022e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272937} :answer-field/id #uuid "b83de849-d036-5a97-bcf3-e053493cb0bf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "818d4509-0f7c-512b-8135-c5e0fbca7d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67580" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{8}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (8) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6212de-bd75-5903-9f74-275c79483650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272938} {:answer/id #uuid "d5995454-1be1-52c0-830c-8d923ecdce0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272939} {:answer/id #uuid "181c16c4-c755-5f1c-a892-14ec71faadf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272940} {:answer/id #uuid "651597aa-a704-59f7-acbc-ae9c1826ac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272941} {:answer/id #uuid "29d72a55-eee5-5409-91de-43e1cdc93c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5995454-1be1-52c0-830c-8d923ecdce0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272939} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9c690e-430c-5984-be11-eb26067258b4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274871}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cd1a104-4645-5dc7-9cad-12182ecbefe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-67622" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $2$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b5b7cefccb376e47581c1e858d8e1afaa707cfa6b87518da42a826ce58dd341c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bae52078-17b6-536d-9cf8-4a92b811c2ae" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8228862d-0567-59f0-8e38-241942cdd44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272943} {:answer/id #uuid "c0adc093-541f-5154-9dd5-2d6abed4c264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272944} {:answer/id #uuid "b8525e90-d10f-517c-a98c-72f6860c3977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272945} {:answer/id #uuid "2dce2de8-8e3a-5715-99e7-e85e3743a959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272946} {:answer/id #uuid "feca2043-a072-58d0-9c6d-ea214a74760c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8525e90-d10f-517c-a98c-72f6860c3977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272945} :answer-field/id #uuid "637b017d-f4cb-5ee4-ae12-6c2378370617" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274872}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ffbfe8-0125-5a08-90b5-155d6aa70b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-67655" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(x-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/91e0a0ebd65df128534d58d181e62be18aa1c398cf526da7838d1a56e62f7d08.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b080170-0f8d-58ab-9074-d6c6c5736b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272978} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d329f8-ae3a-58d8-b468-28dd10f5f175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} {:answer/id #uuid "02041648-13e1-5197-9e59-15673d157025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272980} {:answer/id #uuid "0e005c1b-fa99-54c2-9490-967600ac7c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272981} {:answer/id #uuid "6541c7d4-e447-5aeb-9e32-279d6834beba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d329f8-ae3a-58d8-b468-28dd10f5f175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a3fd53-ba8c-57eb-99bd-051a01bd6968" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274879}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "816839db-3128-5843-a7d7-214ad5efb0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-68092" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $5x≫\\operatorname{ln}(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "736cd524-7c8a-5ee6-beca-d428bcd7f040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273033} {:answer/id #uuid "d06e78de-fe5c-5ca7-af9c-8b3c6a210e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273034} {:answer/id #uuid "88958478-d92c-54dd-9d98-d172c922eee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273035} {:answer/id #uuid "baf29eae-66b4-5a55-b0f8-627d16b6153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 + e^{3})/(e^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273036} {:answer/id #uuid "8abe85f4-c0b5-5881-b79e-6d355e94e711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88958478-d92c-54dd-9d98-d172c922eee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273035} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bc5d8c1-b59a-5d53-9023-097465deb1ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274890}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42630df1-055b-586b-92da-4fb95bf927b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70038" :question/problem "If $2\\operatorname{ln}(x+1)=3+\\operatorname{ln}(x+1)$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}(x+1) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}(x+1) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}(x+1) & =3 \\\\ (2-1)\\operatorname{ln}(x+1) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(x+1) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(x+1) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x+1)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x+1)}=x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & ={e}^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db434d3-8e99-5875-9b26-fd344ea9d89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273048} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dd5029-8052-50f0-90ad-29815adb0a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273049} {:answer/id #uuid "045f129d-a558-52d9-9407-e0eeb621c0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273050} {:answer/id #uuid "f02fc9a8-fd07-5832-936e-81a67083ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 + e^{3})/(e^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273051} {:answer/id #uuid "8cea773f-73c3-5edf-931e-5618817c7931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "045f129d-a558-52d9-9407-e0eeb621c0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273050} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d7f428-ad5f-5e6f-a337-7718ccf44416" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274893}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36687ada-dae6-5a18-a462-3e7164dc30d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-70058" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2)+2\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2)=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2)+2\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =6 \\\\ (1+2)\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =6 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2) & =2 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2)} & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(3x+2)}=3x+2.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & ={e}^{2} \\\\ 3x & ={e}^{2}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81d5fa54-0b80-52c1-97d7-6f8fb231f692" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bd0065-7b2b-54c7-8796-a1cfde4f6fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(12{t}^{2}+6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273188} {:answer/id #uuid "d881a721-9b60-5c4b-9d7d-6d4e6b2299bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(12{t}^{2}+6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273189} {:answer/id #uuid "cbafc927-4022-5cd9-8632-4f9be60a9e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(12{t}^{2}+6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273190} {:answer/id #uuid "9cea3fdd-c241-59c5-afef-9c07123c158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{2}+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273191} {:answer/id #uuid "f98cf01d-b2ae-5fa5-8f7d-057e8def2e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(12{t}^{2}+6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cea3fdd-c241-59c5-afef-9c07123c158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{2}+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273191} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d9abc32-4d2f-58ac-9fc2-7f7c11afb772" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274921}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6e1948c-c265-53e2-82a0-2a4aa4d00659" :question/math-academy-id "q-7100" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+2.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d6ffca0-e1b8-5fe2-9e93-6d99bbf34ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273531} {:answer/id #uuid "e7582abc-2a92-522e-b454-02383a12c516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273532} {:answer/id #uuid "82748ebf-0dfd-5232-94f7-360dbf3b8c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273533} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecbf652-b46b-54a2-b754-802802a435a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273534} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ef2761-8719-5e28-bf55-75af35bae74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6ef2761-8719-5e28-bf55-75af35bae74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1229e0a-d09f-5f76-8c38-aba5f99a9f15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "248ca286-7948-5cc6-99a7-a8138552af97" :question/math-academy-id "q-75638" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $75$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{24}{5}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=75$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{24}{5}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{24}{5})}{75} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{24}{5})(\\frac{1}{75}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =75(\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d74dd4ee-45f1-5ca7-ae6c-9cbb768ac636" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Finite Number of Terms From a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0adf388-9909-53e6-a5cb-176699b204ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261629} {:answer/id #uuid "b99b927d-a15d-5e87-87ff-e4c8ca2814a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b99b927d-a15d-5e87-87ff-e4c8ca2814a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261630} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c70d2c-1a17-5ef9-ae9c-3702bfeb3a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261628}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75e5636b-4302-5507-ac71-dd5e0fb70999" :question/math-academy-id "q-76754" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, we have that ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5414e634-9b33-5dac-9686-4e570efbc7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89da2b14-ec2c-50fe-8c5b-898df5f33de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273646} {:answer/id #uuid "09206c5d-73d3-58bb-ae74-fcbe663f1dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273647} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc732e5-f8f4-5730-98f9-538cbe442165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273648} {:answer/id #uuid "af0c0e54-0afc-5b49-a40d-3055f1cf615f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273649} {:answer/id #uuid "3566d0c5-0e6c-5e74-bfe7-29a551e87e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3566d0c5-0e6c-5e74-bfe7-29a551e87e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273650} :answer-field/id #uuid "f832dab9-ac8c-5e92-a4be-ef9bc0350ea6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f70a3773-bf2a-5b73-abc3-5cb2357d373c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78021" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{e}^{x/5}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fd64db-d24a-542a-853e-c4841fac14a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273799} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd3fe83-ccf9-5bdb-be07-b519fddaf6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273800} {:answer/id #uuid "b34c2ade-cf42-5769-9d15-44f9a290fde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273801} {:answer/id #uuid "2a27529f-f77d-5ed2-9b30-2cde748fbea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34c2ade-cf42-5769-9d15-44f9a290fde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273801} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86543c2-7f6b-592e-b2ba-592983b47cc4" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a143be78-236c-5255-bc5e-3d7bfb88f2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81991" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=10\\operatorname{ln}(5x).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7753c444-3412-5812-a12a-dffabf44b146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273803} {:answer/id #uuid "707396df-f5df-57e9-b4a1-b4dd25374532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3(-\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273804} {:answer/id #uuid "9570cc1e-8306-531f-b085-e6e167b28ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273805} {:answer/id #uuid "451505fd-998a-54cd-94e0-159013acf9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-(-\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273806} {:answer/id #uuid "51f84122-498d-57b8-b6a3-c2bf6415c814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2(-\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9570cc1e-8306-531f-b085-e6e167b28ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273805} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3aa802f-b290-55e5-9852-51b11cb93e4e" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275045}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33d0d905-d5ef-5734-b60d-f9969d67f0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-81993" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}(5x+4)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{2}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210" :knowledge-point/title "The Root of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32fd688-b865-5443-bf1a-0c1bb108d649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273808} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea7d964-3c96-51f6-9296-e4101702e826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273809} {:answer/id #uuid "fd37d675-212d-5131-bc69-ac81e5f69aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273810} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ee11ac-c8f1-5b10-8bf2-b93405fe5a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273811} {:answer/id #uuid "c3877f0e-7a5d-5c58-ad43-b68de1aa899a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ee11ac-c8f1-5b10-8bf2-b93405fe5a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273811} :answer-field/id #uuid "11493611-fdec-5b53-bb5a-a2ed02d61f5d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77ef5dca-afc9-54e0-8284-755f1d588fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81996" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{2}(3x-1)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}(3x-1)-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x-1)-3 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{2}(3x-1) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(3x-1)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ 3x-1 & =8 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210" :knowledge-point/title "The Root of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "517ad057-7c58-5a0e-b87f-83b3194294a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273813} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4af3b8-cba6-5e59-8bdb-ff1d57790eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273814} {:answer/id #uuid "f87df536-71a2-5e4e-a288-a3d4898469be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273815} {:answer/id #uuid "13fadfdc-4708-5a4d-a3ad-beb5f0701821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273816} {:answer/id #uuid "b1389475-6580-5f2f-a71c-0dc4ebfce79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13fadfdc-4708-5a4d-a3ad-beb5f0701821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273816} :answer-field/id #uuid "018a8933-67ef-58af-9cbe-0f0cbd3e2cd3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af0bbf2d-74bd-50a6-bf1f-aad032b60e21" :question/math-academy-id "q-81999" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}(x)-6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210" :knowledge-point/title "The Root of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d6a9dc-6a91-5677-af1a-6f2e73ae2aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{6}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273818} {:answer/id #uuid "1e406bfa-b247-50bc-8c61-d0ce10aaf316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{6}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273819} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd75e0c-c2d0-583a-b707-ca0985a694d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{6}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273820} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ef17c2-3822-55c2-b433-084a79c1717d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{6}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273821} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f2588-f618-5ba6-b72d-1193a32ece40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f2588-f618-5ba6-b72d-1193a32ece40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273822} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b97ad6-9aa1-5a57-94ef-606b926871f5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "116feb8e-6c77-562d-8f5b-52579754920b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82001" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{5}(x-1)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}(x-1)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}(x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{5}(x-1) & =-1 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}(x-1)} & ={5}^{-1} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210" :knowledge-point/title "The Root of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1213d38a-8c74-5a92-aa64-07e7b1bb0b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273823} {:answer/id #uuid "4743b977-8108-5391-bf23-c0d86e3234a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273824} {:answer/id #uuid "05b5468a-bff7-59d5-be4f-0eb7f51a7151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273825} {:answer/id #uuid "98339bfc-0e19-525a-92bb-8cab2a3e33f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273826} {:answer/id #uuid "5c292e3c-a717-5d00-8391-3d86c110cbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b5468a-bff7-59d5-be4f-0eb7f51a7151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273825} :answer-field/id #uuid "092e8b46-6cc3-5ebf-95da-4a8606f0f284" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275049}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55eb1db9-fbcd-5c61-8a62-80c4dc797847" :question/math-academy-id "q-82002" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2-x & =1 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "685caf8b-0c45-5925-988a-ea7ae101733b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273833} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ffab9b-f98c-53e0-baf9-6d561164c8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273834} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c30e8a-0cc9-522d-a9d5-e8dd3717394c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273835} {:answer/id #uuid "dcec60b1-cc4f-513e-97be-f85c1ec1a634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273836} {:answer/id #uuid "93e1c923-254c-5af9-81b5-a147a717cc25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685caf8b-0c45-5925-988a-ea7ae101733b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273833} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef351985-dad1-5d62-89c3-b862d8c1df8c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275051}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a7d0a24-b1bb-5537-9873-f6cb09e77afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-82178" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b6687f-5598-54f5-8d8b-73763091a724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273928} {:answer/id #uuid "823ba39b-277d-5f22-9396-2494c1532282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273929} {:answer/id #uuid "36500270-430a-5679-ae9e-46ef9df8fdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273930} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbd41ce-8061-547c-823d-e8e07ab25b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273931} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c9e8ea-fb0f-5d2e-b547-caa388df0960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "823ba39b-277d-5f22-9396-2494c1532282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273929} :answer-field/id #uuid "99393391-5937-51f9-b320-5ad8f670ac67" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23102c7-5ee9-519d-905e-118a7274280e" :question/math-academy-id "q-90479" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{x}{3}+10).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01be20c8-3c8a-52a9-bcf7-34390867ef90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273933} {:answer/id #uuid "f67730a6-563e-5578-86ae-6a34a3f2a0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273934} {:answer/id #uuid "3437dc7c-21f0-5890-b1f9-e3b199c86862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273935} {:answer/id #uuid "fb75a5d7-db64-5d37-8c85-c34f462ed96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273936} {:answer/id #uuid "77e753de-7b81-5c6b-84bd-abe8e942ddc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f67730a6-563e-5578-86ae-6a34a3f2a0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273934} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad17a8-2107-5daa-bca1-13ee8d562608" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275071}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52cac93d-d305-58e7-b23c-230f64ecb9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-90483" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{log}x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nVertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b153e169-8d41-569a-91f2-4f53841c969a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262025} {:answer/id #uuid "c7769ec2-e466-5eea-8c4a-7bf0a2abde28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262026} {:answer/id #uuid "0872d019-2ad9-51d8-a445-ad0ce08f1417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262027} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1df8e6-3183-51e1-a552-9f34aff2a38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262028} {:answer/id #uuid "d83b6992-3443-5d27-9c0f-2a55881bba6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262029} {:answer/id #uuid "1591b743-d64e-5e9b-979e-bafe889add72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262030} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcdf911-2fd8-5105-811b-a33cc3048e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262031} {:answer/id #uuid "0645d5d5-baad-584c-aff9-0f80ef6607f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7769ec2-e466-5eea-8c4a-7bf0a2abde28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262026} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a2b5171-b74e-56c5-a6ac-c68736954407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17943a85-f5f4-5759-b534-e7aca0b60db9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96676" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{''}+2x{y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\n- Equation III is also a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4d3ce2-71af-592f-8b72-b507088a5597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274048} {:answer/id #uuid "2e315c01-ad7d-507e-8da9-95c8619dd917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274049} {:answer/id #uuid "18fa0e0d-d6b2-54a9-b4c8-d38ea0200c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274050} {:answer/id #uuid "68bd1b3a-bc40-5500-881d-0269461d9190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274051} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9b1fc-f908-5980-ab9b-9101dbeba8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9b1fc-f908-5980-ab9b-9101dbeba8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274052} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7be63e-45ca-5081-850b-74252304b4b0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8915eb7f-9b2d-5186-8942-d8acdc46deaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-99273" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-\\frac{1}{6}$ causes $y=-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffb62492-3cae-5dce-9a2d-a2c53fc046ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274058} {:answer/id #uuid "59b9d9b3-9d82-50c2-8a3f-92ad3afa13f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274059} {:answer/id #uuid "4fdbe047-fe2f-51d8-8bcd-38b820a00ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274060} {:answer/id #uuid "03ef4947-a8f3-56af-8494-2653e58f3c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274061} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fb2f65-5813-5e05-8eb2-a1232964a45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6fb2f65-5813-5e05-8eb2-a1232964a45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274062} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3da2acd-f959-558f-ba83-50c38cdc84e2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275096}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08021ea8-83fb-5aff-bb33-9cc4d8053f5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-99336" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0e8333166f36375ee91ce2fe958683f6f2ab01d02a94fe33ca5ecfec9349a146.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bae52078-17b6-536d-9cf8-4a92b811c2ae" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de75f8f-bfb1-5ebb-928a-d58cdb074b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274083} {:answer/id #uuid "08bf697b-33ca-599a-a821-fae240cc8010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274084} {:answer/id #uuid "def5a4a0-99ef-50f9-961f-62bc1c85b383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274085} {:answer/id #uuid "7586167e-37db-5344-bc07-0fbef238a6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274086} {:answer/id #uuid "af183c59-3271-561d-826e-69a6b7cdc19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def5a4a0-99ef-50f9-961f-62bc1c85b383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274085} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4bd4f1-0ff7-5469-96bb-a5fe2036ba8d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275101}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27db9826-21bb-5812-87cf-b8bcfcbc366a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99662" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2f6ced8942365f93dcff7c4d2b5b902906b913c1959137aeedbe30f907745666.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad10ca6b-d130-559b-a201-15127659de13" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity of a Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fecf85ee-174b-5197-950c-53303980314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274093} {:answer/id #uuid "9c44dd7d-9f0c-5c1a-b152-991bf5ab984c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274094} {:answer/id #uuid "84752eab-65c0-5730-a63a-20d5a317ff1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274095} {:answer/id #uuid "39c34dc6-c59b-50cb-8b51-540673602c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274096} {:answer/id #uuid "91a5f25c-7d04-50d5-9d61-559ae1c4beb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84752eab-65c0-5730-a63a-20d5a317ff1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274095} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff52e00-735d-5492-94c3-d01e6e0a14c4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275103}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3beb4d1c-e100-55af-b4e1-650100e49443" :question/math-academy-id "q-99715" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{4x-5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{4x-5}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/24d694316a99dc9455c772a525c03700dc866b12662d2dc0a90399a7ca44a374.png)\n\nWe see that the function is not defined for $x<\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631dd0e-a893-5099-8b52-e7a543e7f783" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Cube Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c94232-1ebd-5d0d-95bd-b93f6b07180f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274108} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc6fee5-f86e-536c-af11-56bbc66b21b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274109} {:answer/id #uuid "03330096-8603-5249-97c9-5706f7bbf4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274110} {:answer/id #uuid "c520615e-3e08-5436-b6b9-11e8f7acfb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274111} {:answer/id #uuid "e21008de-5b0e-5b72-9d66-b655dbaf5689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c520615e-3e08-5436-b6b9-11e8f7acfb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274111} :answer-field/id #uuid "846f49a6-9700-5694-b529-01475e80c7e2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4794869c-06a9-5c29-93c1-d60c4f9c5809" :question/math-academy-id "q-99782" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{4(-1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0bbfc3e-3847-516f-826d-4a18cf49ccfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $(1)+1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274128} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e593e3-7f6c-5f79-b366-312232601a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274129} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffbb896-442a-5536-b7e1-93b97d4616ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $(1)+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274130} {:answer/id #uuid "df6e89d2-2238-5b17-b0d6-7f9f4558c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $(1)-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274131} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcae7d-b94f-5878-bad4-3de184bc2730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $(1)-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e593e3-7f6c-5f79-b366-312232601a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "right limit at 0 = Does not exist; right limit at 1 = $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274129} :answer-field/id #uuid "1610adb0-aa4f-514e-bf6e-7b70cd779f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "135d935c-2bd7-5669-bc52-6f55c09e6c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99965" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
